00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.920
And to do that we will sort of
go on this side quest to

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.060
understand something related
which is the idea of a linear

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:08.220
transformation,

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:09.660
okay,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.920
and whatever we don't finish of
course we will pick back up with

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.140
on Tuesday.
OK,

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:14.940
so

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:22.860
we

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.940
want to talk about the idea of
the inverse of a matrix.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.900
But before that, we should talk
about the inverse

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:32.020
in contexts which you are
already familiar with.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.520
For example,

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.250
with scalar multiplication.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:42.060
Suppose you needed to solve the
equation 2x equals 5, where x is

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.240
a scalar,

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.920
how would you solve for x here?

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.080
Not a trick question, how would
you solve for x?

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:51.480
Yeah,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.000
I had both sides by two.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.180
Yeah, you would divide both
sides by two,

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.420
but another way of thinking
about this And you space this

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:02.920
out a little is you would
multiply both sides

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.580
By the multiplicative inverse of
two which we know

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:10.840
is a half,

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:13.020
right? And why is it defined
that way?

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.480
Well, it's because 2 times its
multiplicative inverse gives you

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:17.480
what?

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:20.700
Yeah,

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:26.140
1, which is called the identity
element in multiplication, or at

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:27.940
least in scalar multiplication.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:31.980
So this gives me 1x is equal to
5 over 2,

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:32.600
OK?

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:38.560
And this 1 here is called the
identity

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.420
element,

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.100
OK?

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:51.600
So what should a matrix's
inverse do for us?

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.120
Well,

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.950
suppose A is a matrix.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:05.260
What do you think its inverse
should satisfy?

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.880
Following the same pattern here,

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.440
where 2's inverse was the number
you could multiply it by to get

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.200
the identity element, which was
the number 1,

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.540
what should the matrix A's
inverse be?

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:22.500
Yeah? If you multiply A by A,
you get the identity matrix.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:23.500
Good.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:25.840
A's inverse,

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:35.700
which we will denote with the
symbol that looks

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.860
like A to the negative 1,

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:42.340
but it's not a power, it's its
inverse,

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:48.140
should satisfy A times A inverse
is

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:50.680
equal to the identity.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:51.680
Okay?

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:55.080
Now an additional desirable
property will be that it

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:59.660
shouldn't matter which order you
multiply a matrix by its

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:00.320
inverse.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.920
You should get the identity
matrix in either way.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:08.920
So A times A inverse should be A
inverse A as well.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.440
Okay?

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.840
Now but suppose we want both of
these to be We want A times A

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:18.500
inverse to be the identity and A
inverse A to be the identity.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:23.000
What does that require about A's
shape?

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:40.320
Okay, so this idea of an inverse
only applies to square matrices

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:41.320
or

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:51.340
only square matrices have an
inverse.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:55.660
Is that clear?

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:56.660
Okay.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.920
Now, if we do have the inverse
of a square matrix, what would

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:04.420
it allow us to do? Well,

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:11.020
let's say suppose A is N by

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.060
N.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:23.380
The equation Ax equals b, where
x is a vector and b is a vector.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:29.020
I can think of this as a system
of equations and unknowns.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.770
How many equations and unknowns
are there here?

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:41.080
How many equations and how many
unknowns?

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:50.040
Yeah, actually before you answer
that let me have discussed the

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.700
more complicated case which is
where a is not square So suppose

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.200
a is n by d

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:59.020
Then ax equals b

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:03.520
is a system of how many
equations and how many unknowns?

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:04.860
Yeah?

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:08.220
N equations and d unknowns.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:08.580
Good.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:12.330
N equations and d unknowns.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:16.320
So here you have to imagine the
vector x.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:19.640
Its components are x1, x2,

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:21.140
through xd.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:25.340
And a and b are treated as
constants,

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.160
like matrices whose values you
know.

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:28.800
And the question is,

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.300
what vector x

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:34.280
will allow us to multiply into A
and get B?

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:38.080
So what this is really asking us
to do is write B as a linear

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.260
combination of A's columns.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:41.540
Do we agree with that?

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:42.540
OK.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:48.520
This is asking us.

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:00.580
And now,

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:04.330
if there is a solution,

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.900
suppose there is at least one
solution to this.

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:17.150
Suppose there is at least one x
where ax equals b.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:24.230
At least one solution.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.820
What does it tell you about b
and a?

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:30.360
Suppose it's possible to find an
x that Ax equals b.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:35.280
What does that tell you about b
and a?

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.080
Maybe I'll put it like this.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:38.580
b is,

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:43.240
what is true about this vector b

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:48.490
if it's possible to find an x
where Ax equals b?

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:52.360
Yeah?

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:54.800
In the column space? Good.
It's in the column space of a.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.520
Can you remind me what that is?

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:00.000
It's the set of all linear
combinations, base columns.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.000
Exactly.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.700
The column space of A is the set
of everything you can make with

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:05.000
A's columns.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:09.640
If it's possible for you to find
an x where Ax equals b,

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:12.260
then it just means b is a
possible linear combination of

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:13.460
A's columns.

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:14.880
Do we agree with that?

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:15.380
OK.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:18.080
But now if A is a non-square
matrix,

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:21.200
right? On this page, I'm
assuming for a second that it's

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:24.280
not square.
It possibly has more or fewer

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:26.160
rows than columns.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:27.160
Then,

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.660
as we've started to see,

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:31.220
there may be one solution.
There may be infinitely many

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:32.980
solutions.
There may not be a solution at

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:33.980
all.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:38.420
Now, let's suppose we're dealing
with a matrix that is square,

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:39.380
n by n.

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:44.630
This is a system of how many
equations and how many unknowns.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:45.980
Previously,

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:47.380
we had d equations,

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:52.100
sorry, d unknowns, because each
component of the vector x was an

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:53.100
unknown,

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:57.020
and n equations, because each
row of A was an unknown,

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:58.560
or an equation.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:01.060
How many equations and unknowns
do we have here?

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.000
The n equations and unknowns.
Good.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:08.100
n equations and n unknowns.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:13.860
And those unknowns are coming in
the vector x.

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:26.300
What the inverse of A would
allow us to find is the unique

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:30.460
solution x for Ax equals b.

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:34.220
It would allow us to find the
one vector x that when you

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:35.700
multiply it by A,

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:36.980
you get b.

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:40.260
It's like the one ingredient
that tells you how many,

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:42.920
or like the one recipe that
tells you how to make a linear

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:46.560
combination of A's columns to
get b as an output,

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:47.560
okay?

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:52.110
So if A is invertible,

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:58.740
and all that means is this
inverse matrix

00:10:58.740 --> 00:10:59.740
exists,

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:06.600
then I could multiply both sides
of Ax equals B

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:08.850
by A's inverse.

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:15.510
And what would multiplying A
inverse by A give me?

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:21.900
Good, the identity matrix.

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:27.110
And that would just tell me X is
equal to A inverse B.

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:32.740
And this tells me how to make

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:38.640
B as a linear combination

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:45.410
of A's columns.

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:50.740
So that's what we can do if a
matrix is invertible.

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:55.840
If it's invertible, we can find
how to make any possible output

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:58.840
vector using a linear
combination of the inputs.

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:04.460
That's what we will do with a
matrix if we find that it is

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:05.460
invertible.

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:08.600
But the question now is,

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:09.540
well,

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.660
what makes a matrix invertible?

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:14.120
I can just tell you the answer,

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:19.370
but I'd like us to discover it
through this other discovery.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:23.080
But I want you to start thinking
about invertibility through

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:24.180
these lenses.

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:27.740
If a matrix is invertible, then
it has to be the case that you

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:31.640
tell me any vector b in Rn,

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:38.240
And I am able to tell you what x
makes Ax equal to b.

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:40.100
Not every matrix will allow you
to do that.

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:43.220
There will be matrices who are
not invertible, meaning you

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:48.470
won't be able to find an input
for every possible output.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:49.540
OK?

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:53.290
So that's where we're heading.

00:12:54.760 --> 00:13:00.640
But to understand deeply what
makes a matrix

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:04.040
invertible, we need to talk
about this related idea,

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:08.540
which is that of a linear
transformation.

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:11.780
Now a linear transformation, all
it is,

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:15.530
is a type of function.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:23.040
And for a second here, what we
discuss is going to seem like

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:26.020
it's not at all related to what
we've been discussing before.

00:13:26.020 --> 00:13:28.420
It's going to sound like some
new topic, but as we were about

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:29.320
to see,

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:31.900
linear transformations are
closely related to everything

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:35.360
we've studied over the past few
weeks.

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:39.360
What I'll say is suppose T,

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:42.100
capital T, is a function.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:46.720
And capital T is a common letter
that we use in linear algebra to

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:49.720
denote a linear transformation.

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:02.460
What does this notation mean?

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:06.040
T colon rd arrow rn.

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:09.040
What does that mean?

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:12.920
What does that mean?

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:17.620
It's like the function of d
inputs and n outputs.

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:18.920
Yeah,

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:21.980
one way you can think about this
is that the function,

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:26.280
its inputs are vectors with d
things in them, and its outputs

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:28.900
are vectors with n things in
them.

00:14:28.900 --> 00:14:29.900
OK?

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:32.660
Can you remind me, what's the
name for this set,

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:35.080
the set of possible inputs?

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:36.200
Yeah? Yeah,

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:36.660
domain,

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:37.660
good.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:43.180
And the set of possible outputs,
I'll call it's codomain.

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:49.920
Now most functions you've
probably thought of in terms of

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.880
domain and codomain before this
class

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:55.780
probably just had a domain and
codomain of real numbers, or

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:57.900
like some subset of real
numbers.

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:00.740
But as we'll see, functions can
take in a vector and output a

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:01.620
vector too.

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:05.220
But if this seems confusing,
just replace d and n both with

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:07.580
1, and then this is just real
numbers to real numbers,

00:15:07.580 --> 00:15:08.580
OK?

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:15.200
T is a linear transformation

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:21.820
if

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:27.420
it satisfies two

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:28.420
properties.

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:34.480
If you had to guess,

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:38.440
based on the order in which
we've introduced things in this

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:39.440
class,

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:43.200
anytime I've introduced a new
structure and said the structure

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:44.840
supports two properties,

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:48.590
what were those two properties?

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:51.780
What were those two properties?
What was one of them?

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:52.320
Yeah?

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:53.000
Addition.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:54.080
Good.
And what was the other one?

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:56.320
Addition and scalar
multiplication.

00:15:56.320 --> 00:15:58.800
This is not the first time where
I've presented you with

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:00.900
something new and said, OK, this
new thing allows you to do

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:03.150
addition and scalar
multiplication.

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:06.910
What does that mean here?

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:10.780
What does it mean for linear
transformations to support

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:11.760
addition?

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:13.340
It means

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:18.860
that it shouldn't matter

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:23.140
if you add two inputs before
sending them through the

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:27.320
transformation or send them
individually through the

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:29.000
transformation and add the
results.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:30.880
You should get the same result.

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:34.080
So the mental model I have when
thinking about this is you

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:37.080
should imagine the linear
transformation

00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:42.620
as being a machine that all it
does is it

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:44.460
multiplies it, you know,

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:47.100
sorry, I shouldn't say that yet.

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:50.160
It applies whatever function
we're discussing to an input

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:51.340
that you provide.

00:16:51.340 --> 00:16:55.620
So if I pass the input x through
the machine,

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:58.800
what comes back out is t of x.

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:01.880
And so when I say send through
the machine, all I just mean is

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:03.400
call the function on the input.

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:08.650
So what this is saying is for
all possible x and y's

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:13.040
in the domain,

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:17.180
it should be the case that if
you add x and y and send that

00:17:17.180 --> 00:17:21.080
sum through the machine, you get
the same result as if you sent x

00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:22.040
through the machine,

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:24.320
y through the machine, and added
those results.

00:17:24.320 --> 00:17:26.860
Adding before is the same as
adding after,

00:17:26.860 --> 00:17:28.020
OK?

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:31.160
And secondly,

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:34.880
a linear transformation should
support scalar multiplication.

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:40.620
What that means is multiplying
an input by a scalar and then

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:44.480
sending that through the machine
should be the same as sending

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:47.220
the vector without the scalar
attached through the machine and

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:50.970
then multiplying the result by
the scalar.

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:55.740
And that should be true for
every possible x in the domain

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:58.740
and every scalar c.

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:05.940
So if you put properties 1 and 2
together,

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:16.080
what is an easier way of
thinking about this? What is the

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:19.940
bigger pattern that linear
transformations support?

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:26.600
If we can do addition and we can
do scalar multiplication,

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:31.100
what can we do with linear
transformation?

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:38.040
There's a term we've used a lot
this semester.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:41.900
It's made up of two words.
The first one is linear.

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:42.900
Yeah.
Yeah.

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:43.860
Yeah,

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:44.180
yeah.

00:18:44.180 --> 00:18:45.680
Linear combinations.
Linear combinations, yeah.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:51.960
What this says is that T of Ax
plus By

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:58.710
is equal to A times T of X plus
B times T of Y.

00:19:00.340 --> 00:19:05.200
A linear combination of inputs
gets sent to a linear

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:06.740
combination of the outputs.

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:10.490
Not only a linear combination,
but the same linear combination.

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:12.160
Okay?

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:14.580
Now,

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:17.040
what are some examples of linear
transformations? And to keep it

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:20.700
simple, let's start with
functions where the domain and

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:23.460
codomain are just both real
numbers,

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:24.460
R.

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:35.540
f of x equals x squared.

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:39.290
Is this a linear transformation?

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:43.320
No,

00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:45.680
why not?

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:48.400
It feels like it shouldn't,
because there's a squared.

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:50.900
But what I want us to identify
is which properties does it not

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:51.900
satisfy?

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:59.080
One where f of x plus y isn't
equal to x plus y squared.

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:00.360
Good.

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:01.380
Remember,

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:04.240
if it's a linear transformation,
it needs to be the case that for

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:06.960
any inputs x and y,

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:09.880
t of x plus y is equal to t of x
plus t of y.

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:13.240
So if I can find any example
where that doesn't happen,

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:15.460
then I know this is not a linear
transformation.

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:18.480
And I see a squared, so it's
probably not going to happen.

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:19.740
So I can just make up some
numbers.

00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:24.020
OK, so why do we test out 1 and
2?

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:29.440
f of 1 plus 2 is equal to f of
3, which is 9.

00:20:29.440 --> 00:20:35.560
But f of 1 plus f of 2 is equal
to 1 plus 4, which is 5.

00:20:35.560 --> 00:20:37.420
9 is not equal to 5.

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:38.920
So no.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:43.680
The squared part sort of gives
you a hint that this is not

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:45.020
going to be a linear
transformation.

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:47.780
But this is the reason.
OK?

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:53.460
It also doesn't obey the scaling
property, because f of cx is

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:56.260
equal to c squared f of x.

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:57.260
OK?

00:20:57.320 --> 00:20:59.570
So also no.

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:01.480
All right?

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:03.780
So OK, that one's a little
straightforward.

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:09.700
What about f of

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:12.560
x equals 2x plus 5?

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:16.310
Is that a linear transformation?

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:31.940
Is that a linear transformation?

00:21:31.940 --> 00:21:32.940
No,

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:36.080
because the plus 5 and the first
rule just don't work together.

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:37.080
Yes.

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:39.500
It seems like it would be,

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:40.920
right? Because it seems like
this is a line.

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:43.500
and why would a line not be a
linear transformation?

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:44.500
Well,

00:21:45.060 --> 00:21:47.800
this plus 5 actually messes
things up.

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:48.620
Let's see why.

00:21:48.620 --> 00:21:50.880
Remember, if this is a linear
transformation,

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:54.300
it needs to be the case that f
of x plus y equals f of x plus f

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:55.020
of y.

00:21:55.020 --> 00:21:55.640
But let's see.

00:21:55.640 --> 00:21:57.280
Is that true?

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:59.260
Well, let's look at f of 1 plus
2.

00:21:59.260 --> 00:22:02.080
Let's just use the same inputs
as we used in our previous

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:03.080
counterexample.

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:05.360
This should be the same as f of
3,

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:09.780
which is 2 times 3 plus 5, which
is 11.

00:22:09.780 --> 00:22:12.160
What's f of 1 plus f of 2?

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:14.120
It should be equal to 11 as
well.

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:15.120
But is it?

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:17.460
Well f of 1 is 2 times 1 plus 5,

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:21.460
and f of 2 is 2 times 2 plus 5.

00:22:21.460 --> 00:22:24.340
And notice, we still end up
doing 2 times 3,

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:27.500
but we have the plus 5 twice.

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:27.980
Okay?

00:22:27.980 --> 00:22:31.020
So the result isn't 11, but it's
16.

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:35.060
16 is not 11, And so no.

00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:41.040
The plus 5 makes

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:45.480
this not a linear
transformation.

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:55.900
So can you think of a simple
property

00:22:55.900 --> 00:22:58.900
that a linear transformation
needs to satisfy?

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:02.000
A property that's necessary for
a linear transformation to

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:02.840
satisfy,

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:05.090
but not sufficient.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:09.180
That is, every linear
transformation has to do this.

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:11.380
But just because it does this,

00:23:11.380 --> 00:23:15.130
doesn't make it a linear
transformation.

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:21.820
Necessary, but not sufficient.

00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:29.620
For all linear transformations,

00:23:33.820 --> 00:23:37.600
T of what equals what? There's
like a fixed point that every

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:39.850
linear transformation obeys.

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:42.180
Yeah? 0 equals 0.

00:23:42.180 --> 00:23:43.180
Good.

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:45.440
If you send the 0 vector through
the machine,

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:47.380
the 0 vector should come out.

00:23:47.380 --> 00:23:52.540
Why is that? Well look at this
second property.

00:23:52.540 --> 00:23:56.200
It says T of CX should be equal
to C times T of X for every

00:23:56.200 --> 00:23:59.020
possible scalar C in vector X.

00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:02.080
But that means T of 0 times X,

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:03.520
that is T of 0,

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:07.660
should also be equal to 0 times
T of X.

00:24:07.660 --> 00:24:09.120
Do we agree?

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:10.960
So if you send 0 through the
machine,

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:12.340
0 is what should come out.

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:16.680
So anytime you have these plus
scalars,

00:24:16.680 --> 00:24:18.720
you're not going to have a
linear transformation because

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:23.040
it's not going to obey that
scaling property.

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:25.780
Even though we used the
additivity property to show that

00:24:25.780 --> 00:24:26.640
this didn't work,

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:30.300
we could have used the scaling
property as well.

00:24:30.300 --> 00:24:32.980
So this particular
transformation

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:38.810
is what we might call an affine
transformation.

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:42.100
Can you remind me what's an
affine line

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:45.180
and an affine plane? and this is
a term we used earlier.

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:48.860
What was an affine line or
plane?

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:49.860
Yeah.

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:51.880
Exactly,

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:54.460
an affine line was a line that
doesn't necessarily pass through

00:24:54.460 --> 00:24:55.400
the origin.

00:24:55.400 --> 00:24:58.160
An affine plane was a plane that
doesn't necessarily pass through

00:24:58.160 --> 00:24:59.220
the origin.

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:02.600
An affine transformation is a
transformation that otherwise

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:04.940
would be linear, but doesn't
pass through the origin.

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:08.860
So it's like a shifted version
of a linear transformation.

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:11.120
So let's look at some examples
of things that are linear

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:12.380
transformations.
Because we've looked at two

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:14.060
examples of things that are not.

00:25:14.060 --> 00:25:16.240
Well, first, hopefully, you'll
agree with me that if I remove

00:25:16.240 --> 00:25:17.280
the plus 5,

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:20.240
this is a linear transformation.

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:24.020
2 times a plus b is the same as
2a plus 2b,

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:26.600
and the scaling works as well.

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:29.540
But let's look at some more
sophisticated examples,

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:33.290
ones that involve vector inputs
and vector outputs.

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:38.520
I'm going to imagine a
transformation that takes in two

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:43.860
inputs, that is a vector in R2,
and returns a vector in

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:44.860
R3.

00:25:59.980 --> 00:26:02.520
Is this a linear transformation?

00:26:02.520 --> 00:26:02.980
Well,

00:26:02.980 --> 00:26:04.300
looking at it,

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:07.180
it may not be particularly
obvious.

00:26:07.180 --> 00:26:11.040
Like maybe it's possible to come
up with Two inputs where t of x

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:13.580
plus y is not the same as t of x
plus t of y.

00:26:13.580 --> 00:26:16.420
But if you look at this
carefully,

00:26:16.420 --> 00:26:21.100
it turns out that you can write
this as

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:26.500
a matrix times the vector x1,

00:26:26.700 --> 00:26:27.700
x2.

00:26:31.700 --> 00:26:33.060
And if we can do that,

00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:36.810
then some nice properties of
matrix-vector multiplication

00:26:37.060 --> 00:26:39.310
automatically will apply.

00:26:40.120 --> 00:26:44.080
Can you think of how to write
this linear transformation, the

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:46.960
one that takes in a vector with
components x1 and x2,

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:50.600
and returns the vector x1 plus
x2,

00:26:50.600 --> 00:26:51.600
2x1,

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:54.300
and 3x1 minus 4x2?

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:58.800
Can we think of what matrix
would send x to this new vector?

00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:02.840
Yeah, one,

00:27:03.140 --> 00:27:03.940
one,

00:27:03.940 --> 00:27:05.440
two, zero,

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:07.040
three, negative four.

00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:08.040
Awesome.

00:27:10.580 --> 00:27:12.760
Let's work in reverse here.

00:27:12.760 --> 00:27:16.240
If I multiply this matrix A by
the vector x1, x2,

00:27:16.240 --> 00:27:19.560
well then the first component of
the output will be 1 times x1

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:21.020
plus 1 times x2,

00:27:21.020 --> 00:27:22.940
which is x1 plus x2.

00:27:22.940 --> 00:27:27.540
The second component will be 2
times x1 plus 0 times x2, which

00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:28.920
is just 2 times x1.

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:33.360
And the third component will be
3 times x1 minus 4 times x2,

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:38.610
which is 3x1 minus 4x2, which is
what we have here.

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:42.800
Do we see that?

00:27:43.340 --> 00:27:49.280
So this transformation is really
just a matrix times a

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:50.280
vector.

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:57.200
And matrix-vector multiplication
obeys or supports

00:27:57.200 --> 00:27:59.260
addition and scalar
multiplication.

00:27:59.260 --> 00:28:00.760
It's distributive,

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:01.980
right?

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:05.800
So things like t of x plus y,

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:09.780
I know that that is going to be
equal to t of x plus t of y.

00:28:09.780 --> 00:28:10.580
Why is that?

00:28:10.580 --> 00:28:14.520
Well, it's because t of x plus y
is the same as multiplying the

00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:16.480
sum x plus y by a,

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:18.560
but this operation is
distributive.

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:22.940
This is the same as ax plus ay.

00:28:22.940 --> 00:28:28.940
But that's the same as t of x
plus t of y.

00:28:30.180 --> 00:28:32.960
So if we can write our function

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:36.240
in the form of matrix times
vector,

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:38.900
well then we know it will be a
linear transformation.

00:28:38.900 --> 00:28:43.440
Because the act of multiplying a
vector by a matrix,

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:46.480
that is distributive.
And that supports linear

00:28:46.480 --> 00:28:47.480
combinations.

00:28:48.580 --> 00:28:51.700
If you want to see the other
property written down,

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:56.200
T of C times X is the same as A
times C of X, where A is now a

00:28:56.200 --> 00:28:57.200
matrix.

00:28:57.880 --> 00:29:00.340
I can pull the scalar up front.

00:29:00.340 --> 00:29:02.600
This is C times A times X,

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:06.700
but that's just C times T of X.

00:29:06.700 --> 00:29:09.780
This notation here, it may be a
little confusing because

00:29:09.780 --> 00:29:13.040
here T is a function and A is a
matrix.

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:15.160
And I'm using parentheses after
both of them, But the

00:29:15.160 --> 00:29:16.740
parentheses are doing different
things.

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:19.740
The first parentheses, t
parentheses x plus y,

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:22.280
that's because t is a function
and I'm calling it.

00:29:22.280 --> 00:29:25.960
The second parentheses is to
denote multiplication,

00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:30.460
which is why there aren't any
here because I don't have a sum.

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:34.240
OK, are we clear on how I was
able to write my linear

00:29:34.240 --> 00:29:37.240
transformation as a matrix times
a vector?

00:29:37.240 --> 00:29:39.860
And because I was able to do
that,

00:29:39.860 --> 00:29:43.280
then I know this function is a
linear transformation Because it

00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:46.000
will automatically obeys the
properties that I said a linear

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:48.620
transformation had to obey
earlier

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:53.120
Okay, so that's the big idea

00:29:58.160 --> 00:30:00.540
All linear transformations

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:10.120
Are matrix vector
multiplications

00:30:15.860 --> 00:30:19.460
So in general, you can think of
a linear transformation as

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:23.960
taking in a vector x and
returning Ax.

00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:30.140
And now this hopefully gives
some context for why I said the

00:30:30.140 --> 00:30:33.300
domain of a linear
transformation in general was Rd

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:37.050
and the codomain was Rn.

00:30:37.280 --> 00:30:40.180
Typically, when I have a matrix
A,

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:42.740
which is n by d,

00:30:42.740 --> 00:30:46.380
what must the shape of x be for
it to be legal to multiply A by

00:30:46.380 --> 00:30:47.380
x?

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:51.220
How many things were allowed to
be in x?

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:51.620
Yeah?

00:30:51.620 --> 00:30:52.500
d by 1.

00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:55.600
Yeah, x had to be d by 1.
Or in other words, the x that

00:30:55.600 --> 00:31:01.000
I'm sending through this machine
had to be a vector in Rd,

00:31:01.940 --> 00:31:04.920
because it had to be legal to
multiply at a by x in the first

00:31:04.920 --> 00:31:05.920
place.

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:12.790
So that's why Rd was the domain.

00:31:14.540 --> 00:31:16.380
What comes out?

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:21.630
That is, what is the shape of
the vector Ax?

00:31:23.560 --> 00:31:28.060
How many things are in the
vector Ax?

00:31:28.760 --> 00:31:29.760
Yeah.

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:32.400
Yeah.
The shape is n by 1 or it's a

