00:46:26.820 --> 00:46:29.900
of it as a linear combination of
the matrix's columns.

00:46:29.900 --> 00:46:34.900
This is just saying 1 of A's
first column plus 0 of A's

00:46:34.900 --> 00:46:37.300
second column is equal to 15, 4.

00:46:37.300 --> 00:46:40.980
That's just telling you A's
first column is 15, 4.

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:42.480
We agree?

00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:44.540
Just from this information
alone,

00:46:44.540 --> 00:46:49.040
you know that A's first column
is 15, 4.

00:46:49.780 --> 00:46:52.640
Do we agree with that?

00:46:52.640 --> 00:46:54.260
Yeah.
Can you explain that again?

00:46:54.500 --> 00:46:55.100
OK.
OK,

00:46:55.100 --> 00:46:58.200
what does multiplying a by 1, 0
do?

00:46:58.200 --> 00:46:59.440
Tell me then.

00:46:59.440 --> 00:47:01.740
It multiplies the first column
into one of the second columns.

00:47:01.740 --> 00:47:02.560
Exactly.

00:47:02.560 --> 00:47:05.540
But 1 of the first column plus 0
of the second column is just

00:47:05.540 --> 00:47:07.480
equal to the first column.

00:47:07.480 --> 00:47:11.000
And so this is just telling you
the first column is 15, 4.

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:12.000
Good.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:15.280
So 15,

00:47:15.280 --> 00:47:16.280
4.

00:47:17.640 --> 00:47:19.640
It also tells us what the second
column is.

00:47:19.640 --> 00:47:21.120
Because this is saying,

00:47:21.120 --> 00:47:25.560
if you multiply A by 0, 1,

00:47:25.560 --> 00:47:28.440
which, again, all it does is it
takes none of the first column

00:47:28.440 --> 00:47:31.600
and one of the second, which is
just equal to the second column,

00:47:31.600 --> 00:47:34.740
you get 6, negative 2.

00:47:34.740 --> 00:47:36.040
So what's my point?

00:47:36.040 --> 00:47:39.680
If you know what happens to the
vectors 1, 0, and 0, 1,

00:47:39.680 --> 00:47:41.760
you can find the matrix
associated with the

00:47:41.760 --> 00:47:44.460
transformation, which means then
you can find what happens to any

00:47:44.460 --> 00:47:45.460
input.

00:47:47.740 --> 00:47:49.800
And these vectors,

00:47:49.800 --> 00:47:53.550
1, 0, and 0, 1,

00:47:54.080 --> 00:47:57.830
are called the standard basis
vectors.

00:48:01.320 --> 00:48:04.600
In R2, there are two standard
basis vectors, 1, 0, 0, 1.

00:48:04.600 --> 00:48:06.260
In R3, there are three,

00:48:06.260 --> 00:48:07.420
1, 0, 0,

00:48:07.420 --> 00:48:11.600
0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1.
R1 and R15, there are 15

00:48:11.600 --> 00:48:15.350
standard basis vectors and so
on.

00:48:15.560 --> 00:48:17.440
Now with that in mind,

00:48:17.440 --> 00:48:19.760
let's take a look at this first
visual that I showed you a few

00:48:19.760 --> 00:48:21.000
minutes ago.

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:25.540
What I've drawn on the left are
the two standard basis vectors

00:48:25.540 --> 00:48:26.700
in R2,

00:48:26.700 --> 00:48:30.360
which for this demonstration I
will call U1,

00:48:30.360 --> 00:48:32.100
which is the first one, one
zero,

00:48:32.100 --> 00:48:33.960
and U2, which is the second one,

00:48:33.960 --> 00:48:35.160
zero one.

00:48:35.160 --> 00:48:40.880
And what I've drawn for you is
the result of sending just

00:48:40.880 --> 00:48:45.380
U1 and U2 through the linear
transformation.

00:48:45.960 --> 00:48:48.400
And again, sending through the
transformation

00:48:48.400 --> 00:48:52.150
just means multiplying by A.

00:48:53.680 --> 00:48:57.100
And what do we see about the
result?

00:48:57.100 --> 00:48:57.660
Well,

00:48:57.660 --> 00:49:01.240
what this is showing us is what
the linear transformation does

00:49:01.240 --> 00:49:04.000
to each column of A
individually,

00:49:04.000 --> 00:49:07.180
or sorry, each standard basis
vector individually.

00:49:07.180 --> 00:49:11.180
So what happened to the first
standard basis vector, u1?

00:49:11.180 --> 00:49:14.180
The vector 1, 0.
What happened to it?

00:49:17.400 --> 00:49:19.660
What happened to it? You got
double?

00:49:19.660 --> 00:49:25.340
Yeah, I got stretched by a
factor of 2.

00:49:25.880 --> 00:49:30.380
And what happened to the second
standard basis vector?

00:49:38.120 --> 00:49:41.870
What happened to the second
standard basis vector?

00:49:42.620 --> 00:49:45.220
One third of the length and it
points in the other direction.

00:49:45.220 --> 00:49:46.220
Yeah.

00:49:46.280 --> 00:49:48.660
It's one third of the length and
now points in the other

00:49:48.660 --> 00:49:49.660
direction.

00:49:50.880 --> 00:49:53.940
So what I'm trying to get at
here is understanding what

00:49:53.940 --> 00:49:57.340
happens to the two standard
basis vectors helps us

00:49:57.340 --> 00:50:00.320
understand what happens to any
arbitrary vector.

00:50:00.320 --> 00:50:01.220
Okay,

00:50:01.220 --> 00:50:04.160
if these are how our building
blocks change,

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:07.560
then we can use this to
understand how any arbitrary

00:50:07.560 --> 00:50:09.060
input changes.

00:50:09.180 --> 00:50:11.520
Here's what I mean.

00:50:11.520 --> 00:50:17.520
Suppose I start with the vector
AB and I send it

00:50:17.520 --> 00:50:23.220
through the transformation and
what comes out is the vector

00:50:23.220 --> 00:50:28.480
that is transformed.

00:50:30.700 --> 00:50:34.450
But the vector that is
transformed,

00:50:35.620 --> 00:50:37.000
maybe I'll think of it this way,

00:50:37.000 --> 00:50:38.280
is vector AB

00:50:38.280 --> 00:50:42.800
is really just A times the first
standard basis vector

00:50:42.800 --> 00:50:45.360
plus B times the second standard
basis vector.

00:50:45.360 --> 00:50:49.860
This is just a complicated way
of writing the vector AB.

00:50:50.320 --> 00:50:52.720
Do you agree with that?

00:50:52.720 --> 00:50:58.300
This is just saying any vector
a, b is a times 1, 0 plus b

00:50:58.300 --> 00:50:59.540
times 0,

00:50:59.540 --> 00:51:00.400
1.

00:51:00.400 --> 00:51:03.480
Are we 100% OK with that?

00:51:03.480 --> 00:51:07.980
When I send that through the
transformation,

00:51:08.060 --> 00:51:09.720
as we saw before,

00:51:09.720 --> 00:51:14.120
the result is a times t of u1

00:51:14.120 --> 00:51:17.580
plus b times t of u2.

00:51:17.580 --> 00:51:23.080
So if we figure out how u1 and
u2 get

00:51:23.080 --> 00:51:24.080
transformed,

00:51:24.680 --> 00:51:28.280
then the vector ab gets
transformed to a times the

00:51:28.280 --> 00:51:31.780
output of the first standard
basis vector plus b times the

00:51:31.780 --> 00:51:35.530
output of the second standard
basis vector.

00:51:35.880 --> 00:51:41.860
So with t of x being equal

00:51:41.860 --> 00:51:45.660
to the matrix we saw in the
previous slide,

00:51:45.660 --> 00:51:46.160
2,

00:51:46.160 --> 00:51:51.410
0, 0, negative a third times x,

00:51:53.080 --> 00:51:57.720
T of AB ends up being equal to A

00:51:57.720 --> 00:52:02.220
times T of U1 plus B

00:52:02.840 --> 00:52:05.120
times T of U2,

00:52:05.120 --> 00:52:10.080
which is equal to A times not 1,
0, but now 2, 0, because that's

00:52:10.080 --> 00:52:11.800
what happens to the first
standard basis factor.

00:52:11.800 --> 00:52:16.300
Maybe I'll put that in red.

00:52:22.360 --> 00:52:26.080
Plus b times the transformed
second standard basis vector,

00:52:26.080 --> 00:52:27.000
which is 0,

00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:29.220
negative 1 third.

00:52:29.220 --> 00:52:29.960
OK?

00:52:29.960 --> 00:52:31.740
And again, I'm not trying to
give you an easy way to

00:52:31.740 --> 00:52:33.760
calculate the output.
Of course, the easy way to

00:52:33.760 --> 00:52:35.640
calculate the output, as we will
see,

00:52:35.640 --> 00:52:38.260
is you just multiply the first
component by 2 and the second by

00:52:38.260 --> 00:52:39.380
negative 1 third.

00:52:39.380 --> 00:52:41.740
I'm trying to give you a way of
interpreting what happens

00:52:41.740 --> 00:52:43.820
through the linear
transformation,

00:52:43.820 --> 00:52:46.640
which will be useful when we
look at more complicated linear

00:52:46.640 --> 00:52:47.640
transformations.

00:52:48.600 --> 00:52:51.600
So what we're saying here is
that previously the vector we

00:52:51.600 --> 00:52:55.300
started with was just A of the
first standard basis vector plus

00:52:55.300 --> 00:52:56.880
B of the second standard basis
vector.

00:52:56.880 --> 00:52:58.360
It was just the vector AB,

00:52:58.360 --> 00:53:00.700
one, two, or seven, five, or
something like that.

00:53:00.700 --> 00:53:02.780
Now instead of being one, two,
or seven,

00:53:02.780 --> 00:53:03.380
five,

00:53:03.380 --> 00:53:08.680
it's one times the transformed
standard basis vector plus 2

00:53:08.680 --> 00:53:11.880
times the next transform
standard basis vector.

00:53:12.400 --> 00:53:14.560
So it used to be a linear
combination of the original

00:53:14.560 --> 00:53:15.680
building blocks.

00:53:15.680 --> 00:53:18.180
Now it's a linear combination of
these transformed building

00:53:18.180 --> 00:53:19.180
blocks.

00:53:19.980 --> 00:53:21.780
You kind of see what I mean by
that?

00:53:21.780 --> 00:53:24.200
So understanding what happens to
the standard building blocks

00:53:24.200 --> 00:53:27.460
tells us what happens to any
other vector.

00:53:27.460 --> 00:53:31.640
That's why we're visualizing the
transformation that's happening

00:53:31.640 --> 00:53:35.390
to the standard basis blocks.

00:53:37.340 --> 00:53:39.960
Now, let's think about how we
would describe this

00:53:39.960 --> 00:53:42.210
transformation in English.

00:53:46.760 --> 00:53:50.140
What does this transformation do
to an arbitrary vector,

00:53:50.140 --> 00:53:53.890
like the vector 2, 2?

00:53:56.260 --> 00:54:01.510
In English, what does it do to
it?

00:54:10.550 --> 00:54:15.800
What does this matrix do to an
arbitrary vector?

00:54:23.020 --> 00:54:23.740
Well,

00:54:23.740 --> 00:54:28.000
to see, we can multiply it out.

00:54:28.000 --> 00:54:31.750
What is the vector AV?

00:54:32.880 --> 00:54:34.860
What comes out?

00:54:34.860 --> 00:54:36.100
Negative 2 thirds.

00:54:36.100 --> 00:54:38.180
4 and negative 2 thirds.

00:54:38.180 --> 00:54:42.420
And how would you describe the
process of changing 2, 2 to 4

00:54:42.420 --> 00:54:46.170
and negative 2 thirds? Like,
what did we do?

00:54:47.760 --> 00:54:48.760
Yeah?

00:54:49.020 --> 00:54:51.320
Stretched it and multiply it by
2,

00:54:51.320 --> 00:54:55.820
and then we multiply the y
component by negative 1 third.

00:54:56.280 --> 00:54:57.220
Good.

00:54:57.220 --> 00:55:01.540
So what A does is it stretches,
or compresses,

00:55:01.540 --> 00:55:03.040
or rotates,

00:55:03.280 --> 00:55:04.040
not rotates,

00:55:04.040 --> 00:55:05.040
reflects,

00:55:08.040 --> 00:55:12.000
each component independently.

00:55:15.780 --> 00:55:17.620
What do I mean by independently?

00:55:17.620 --> 00:55:20.660
The thing that I did to the y
component, which is multiplied

00:55:20.660 --> 00:55:22.240
by negative 1 third,

00:55:22.240 --> 00:55:25.020
was unrelated to the thing I did
to the x component, which is

00:55:25.020 --> 00:55:26.020
double.

00:55:26.560 --> 00:55:30.140
The x component and y component
were transformed totally

00:55:30.140 --> 00:55:31.140
separately.

00:55:32.040 --> 00:55:35.660
You can imagine a transformation
that uses both the x and

00:55:35.660 --> 00:55:38.960
y-coordinates and somehow uses
the joint position to configure

00:55:38.960 --> 00:55:41.700
where the output will be.
We'll see that in just a moment

00:55:41.700 --> 00:55:44.120
when we talk about a rotation.

00:55:44.120 --> 00:55:46.960
But what this matrix is doing is
it's just separately doing

00:55:46.960 --> 00:55:49.720
something to x and separately
doing something to y.

00:55:49.720 --> 00:55:52.360
And the doing something is just
multiplying it by a scalar.

00:55:52.360 --> 00:55:55.100
If it's a scalar greater than 1,
it stretches it.

00:55:55.100 --> 00:55:57.920
It's a scalar between 0 and 1.
It compresses it.

00:55:57.920 --> 00:56:01.840
If it's a negative scalar, then
it also flips its direction.

00:56:01.840 --> 00:56:04.760
So 2, 2 gets sent to

00:56:04.760 --> 00:56:08.820
4 negative 2 thirds.

00:56:09.620 --> 00:56:15.420
So the output vector is going to
look something like that.

00:56:19.820 --> 00:56:20.500
Now,

00:56:20.500 --> 00:56:23.360
there is a name for this kind of
matrix that we've discussed.

00:56:23.360 --> 00:56:27.860
What would you call this matrix?

00:56:33.640 --> 00:56:37.980
And it has to do with the fact
that the new x component only

00:56:37.980 --> 00:56:39.680
depended on the old x component,

00:56:39.680 --> 00:56:43.380
and the new y component only
depended on the old y component.

00:56:43.380 --> 00:56:45.420
What was the name for this
matrix?

00:56:45.420 --> 00:56:48.080
What is a rotation matrix?

00:56:48.080 --> 00:56:48.900
Not quite.

00:56:48.900 --> 00:56:52.620
We haven't yet discussed about a
rotation matrix.

00:56:52.620 --> 00:56:55.460
This is not what I would say is
a rotation matrix.

00:56:55.460 --> 00:56:59.960
It's a diagonal? It's a diagonal
matrix.

00:57:07.660 --> 00:57:10.720
And sorry, to make more clear
what I mean by it affects each

00:57:10.720 --> 00:57:12.220
component individually,

00:57:12.700 --> 00:57:14.960
if I transform the vector AB,

00:57:14.960 --> 00:57:17.600
what is the result?

00:57:17.600 --> 00:57:21.380
It's 2A and negative B over 3.

00:57:21.380 --> 00:57:24.860
There's not a B up here, and
there's not an A down here.

00:57:24.860 --> 00:57:28.140
The new A only depends on the
old A, And the new B only

00:57:28.140 --> 00:57:31.040
depends on the old B.

00:57:31.040 --> 00:57:34.520
And that's happening because
these off-diagonal terms are

00:57:34.520 --> 00:57:35.520
zeros.

00:57:36.200 --> 00:57:37.880
When you have a diagonal matrix,

00:57:37.880 --> 00:57:40.200
each component is just scaled
independently.

00:57:40.200 --> 00:57:43.020
So you should think of a
diagonal matrix's job as being

00:57:43.020 --> 00:57:46.760
to stretch or compress each
direction on its own.

00:57:46.760 --> 00:57:49.240
This is an idea we will use a
lot this semester.

00:57:49.240 --> 00:57:53.740
A diagonal matrix is used for
stretching.

00:57:55.380 --> 00:57:58.560
Okay, we've briefly discussed a
rotation matrix, and you've seen

00:57:58.560 --> 00:58:00.420
them in your homework.

00:58:00.420 --> 00:58:03.580
Here's what a rotation matrix
might look like.

00:58:03.580 --> 00:58:09.200
So what we're seeing is a matrix
B, and on the left, again, I

00:58:09.200 --> 00:58:12.980
have the standard basis vectors
u1 and u2, 1, 0, and 0, 1.

00:58:13.740 --> 00:58:18.580
And on the right, what I've seen
is how they get transformed.

00:58:18.580 --> 00:58:21.580
And how were they transformed?

00:58:21.580 --> 00:58:26.220
They were rotated 45 degrees
counterclockwise.

00:58:37.680 --> 00:58:41.430
Now in the homework you
submitted on Sunday,

00:58:41.860 --> 00:58:46.360
there was a property of rotation
matrices

00:58:47.020 --> 00:58:52.840
that you proved it was that the
length of

00:58:52.840 --> 00:58:58.100
say B is our rotation matrix and
V is our vector the length of B

00:58:58.100 --> 00:59:00.760
V is equal to the length of
something else

00:59:00.760 --> 00:59:06.680
what is it equal

00:59:06.680 --> 00:59:12.520
to the length the length of B V
is equal to yeah V it's equal to

00:59:12.520 --> 00:59:14.120
the length of v.

00:59:14.120 --> 00:59:20.000
What that means is if we started
with the vector 2, 2,

00:59:23.120 --> 00:59:25.620
the result we get back, which is
going to look something like

00:59:25.620 --> 00:59:26.620
this,

00:59:28.480 --> 00:59:30.800
is going to have the same
length.

00:59:30.800 --> 00:59:34.060
All that's going to be different
is its angle with the origin is

00:59:34.060 --> 00:59:36.840
going to be changed because it's
being rotated by whatever amount

00:59:36.840 --> 00:59:38.840
b tells us to rotate by.

00:59:38.840 --> 00:59:41.320
Not true here.
Right here,

00:59:41.600 --> 00:59:42.260
A, V,

00:59:42.260 --> 00:59:44.640
and V don't have to have the
same length.

00:59:44.640 --> 00:59:48.060
And in general, they won't,
because we're stretching each

00:59:48.060 --> 00:59:50.100
component by a different amount.

00:59:50.100 --> 00:59:52.350
In this rotation,

00:59:52.740 --> 00:59:54.500
the length of the vector is not
changing.

00:59:54.500 --> 00:59:57.860
It's just that its direction is
changing.

00:59:57.860 --> 01:00:03.110
And can you also remind me what
the name of B is as a matrix?

01:00:04.460 --> 01:00:07.440
Its action is that it rotates,
but there is like an algebraic

01:00:07.440 --> 01:00:09.420
property that we said it had.

01:00:09.420 --> 01:00:12.780
And what was that algebraic
property?

01:00:12.780 --> 01:00:17.580
It was a word that started with
an O.

01:00:17.580 --> 01:00:18.320
Yeah?

01:00:18.320 --> 01:00:18.940
Orthogonal?

01:00:18.940 --> 01:00:22.690
Yeah, it was an orthogonal
matrix.

01:00:23.800 --> 01:00:26.000
Can you remind me what the
properties of an orthogonal

01:00:26.000 --> 01:00:27.500
matrix are?

01:00:33.640 --> 01:00:35.140
Yeah? Yeah.

01:00:35.200 --> 01:00:37.840
All the vectors are unit
vectors.

01:00:37.840 --> 01:00:38.840
Good.

01:00:40.220 --> 01:00:44.620
Did you dot the rows with the
columns?

01:00:44.620 --> 01:00:46.420
Close.
It's that you dot the columns

01:00:46.420 --> 01:00:47.920
with themselves,

01:00:48.040 --> 01:00:49.420
you either get 1 or 0.

01:00:49.420 --> 01:00:52.060
If you dot a column with itself,
you get 1,

01:00:52.060 --> 01:00:53.520
because that means it's a unit
vector.

01:00:53.520 --> 01:00:55.700
The dot product of the vector
with itself is its squared

01:00:55.700 --> 01:00:56.500
length,

01:00:56.500 --> 01:00:59.040
and if that's equal to 1, then
its squared length is 1,

01:00:59.040 --> 01:01:00.960
which means its length is also
1.

01:01:00.960 --> 01:01:03.200
And if you dot a column with a
different column, you get 0,

01:01:03.200 --> 01:01:06.950
which means the columns are
orthogonal.

01:01:07.080 --> 01:01:09.880
So it means B transpose B, which
is,

01:01:09.880 --> 01:01:13.000
remember, the dot products of
the pairs of columns is equal to

01:01:13.000 --> 01:01:14.500
the identity.

01:01:18.520 --> 01:01:22.260
But using things we just
introduced today,

01:01:22.260 --> 01:01:27.860
what this means is that B
transpose is B's

01:01:27.860 --> 01:01:28.860
inverse.

01:01:29.520 --> 01:01:32.860
Because I found a matrix that I
can multiply B by to get the

01:01:32.860 --> 01:01:33.860
identity.

01:01:34.560 --> 01:01:38.980
So B and B transpose are
inverses of each other, which is

01:01:38.980 --> 01:01:42.680
a special case when the columns
are unit vectors

01:01:42.680 --> 01:01:44.140
and orthogonal to each other.

01:01:44.140 --> 01:01:44.920
In general,

01:01:44.920 --> 01:01:47.380
a matrix's inverse is not the
same as its transpose.

01:01:47.380 --> 01:01:49.440
It's true in this particular
case.

01:01:49.440 --> 01:01:53.280
But because an inverse can be
applied in either direction,

01:01:53.280 --> 01:01:57.780
this also means that B B
transpose is the identity.

01:01:58.280 --> 01:02:00.820
Again, in general, it's not true
that B transpose B and B B

01:02:00.820 --> 01:02:02.420
transpose are the same,

01:02:02.420 --> 01:02:03.080
right?

01:02:03.080 --> 01:02:05.080
If B is not a square matrix,

01:02:05.080 --> 01:02:07.280
then B transpose B and BB
transpose don't even have the

01:02:07.280 --> 01:02:08.400
same shape.

01:02:08.400 --> 01:02:12.360
But when they're square and the
matrix is an orthogonal matrix,

01:02:12.360 --> 01:02:16.140
then these are both the same.

01:02:16.140 --> 01:02:19.780
OK, so the role of an orthogonal
matrix is to rotate.

01:02:19.780 --> 01:02:22.220
That's something that you should
keep in mind.

01:02:22.220 --> 01:02:24.940
And we will see repeatedly
throughout the semester.

01:02:24.940 --> 01:02:27.560
What happens to a vector when
multiplied by an orthogonal

01:02:27.560 --> 01:02:28.400
matrix?

01:02:28.400 --> 01:02:31.400
Its length doesn't change.
it just rotates.

01:02:32.640 --> 01:02:33.640
OK.

01:02:33.680 --> 01:02:34.680
Now,

01:02:36.480 --> 01:02:38.380
we've seen two examples,

01:02:38.380 --> 01:02:40.300
a matrix that stretches

01:02:40.300 --> 01:02:42.880
and a matrix that rotates.

01:02:42.880 --> 01:02:45.060
What happens if we apply both of
them?

01:02:45.060 --> 01:02:45.640
Sorry.

