01:14:57.160 --> 01:14:59.560
And here's another reason it
can't be inverted.

01:14:59.560 --> 01:15:03.160
Suppose I give you a possible
output,

01:15:03.160 --> 01:15:05.740
like this vector,

01:15:07.580 --> 01:15:09.700
negative 2, 2.

01:15:09.700 --> 01:15:10.740
And I said,

01:15:10.740 --> 01:15:11.500
hey,

01:15:11.500 --> 01:15:15.980
this came out of the machine
where the machine, all it does

01:15:15.980 --> 01:15:19.280
is it takes a vector and
multiplies it by 1, 0, 0, 0.

01:15:19.280 --> 01:15:20.220
And I say, OK,

01:15:20.220 --> 01:15:21.380
this is the vector

01:15:21.380 --> 01:15:22.980
that came out of the machine.

01:15:22.980 --> 01:15:26.730
What would you tell me?

01:15:30.500 --> 01:15:33.000
I say, OK, I have this machine
where all it does is it

01:15:33.000 --> 01:15:35.320
multiplies vectors by 1, 0, 0,
0.

01:15:35.320 --> 01:15:38.280
And I claim this vector that
I've drawn in purple, Well,

01:15:38.280 --> 01:15:40.140
negative 2, 2 is what came out.

01:15:40.140 --> 01:15:43.140
What would you say?

01:15:49.620 --> 01:15:52.800
Vectors not linearly on
combinations,

01:15:53.200 --> 01:15:55.720
because you have to add the 2,
2s,

01:15:55.720 --> 01:15:58.280
the 0, 2 on to that, whatever
comes out.

01:15:58.280 --> 01:16:01.300
So what would you say? Is this a
possible thing that could come

01:16:01.300 --> 01:16:03.180
out of the machine?

01:16:03.180 --> 01:16:04.180
No.

01:16:04.420 --> 01:16:04.880
You'd say,

01:16:04.880 --> 01:16:05.300
OK,

01:16:05.300 --> 01:16:06.020
you're lying,

01:16:06.020 --> 01:16:11.080
because that can't happen.
Right? This is not a possible

01:16:11.080 --> 01:16:12.800
output.

01:16:17.180 --> 01:16:18.180
Okay?

01:16:18.320 --> 01:16:22.560
So what we're seeing is that for
this matrix,

01:16:22.560 --> 01:16:26.380
there are multiple reasons the
associated transformation can't

01:16:26.380 --> 01:16:27.600
be undone.

01:16:27.600 --> 01:16:30.860
One reason is that it sends
multiple inputs to the same

01:16:30.860 --> 01:16:31.860
output.

01:16:32.160 --> 01:16:34.340
In fact, it sends infinitely
many inputs to the same output.

01:16:34.340 --> 01:16:37.180
There are infinitely many
vectors that all get sent to the

01:16:37.180 --> 01:16:38.800
vector 2, 0.

01:16:38.800 --> 01:16:39.400
And then,

01:16:39.400 --> 01:16:42.340
for lots of possible outputs,

01:16:42.340 --> 01:16:44.820
there's no input that sends us
there,

01:16:44.820 --> 01:16:45.820
OK?

01:16:47.080 --> 01:16:51.580
What do you notice about the
columns of this matrix?

01:16:53.480 --> 01:16:56.480
Are they linearly independent?

01:16:56.560 --> 01:16:57.220
No.

01:16:57.220 --> 01:17:00.300
Any time the 0 vector is one of
the vectors in your collection,

01:17:00.300 --> 01:17:02.100
the collection is not linearly
independent.

01:17:02.100 --> 01:17:05.440
calls not linearly independent.

01:17:07.020 --> 01:17:10.020
Here's another such example.

01:17:11.380 --> 01:17:15.130
Actually I didn't draw the
example.

01:17:16.020 --> 01:17:18.270
Here you go.

01:17:21.860 --> 01:17:24.600
Here's a matrix H where the
calls are not linearly

01:17:24.600 --> 01:17:25.600
independent.

01:17:26.960 --> 01:17:30.900
And what you see is that the
transformed standard basis

01:17:30.900 --> 01:17:34.220
vectors still live on a line.

01:17:34.220 --> 01:17:37.000
It's not the x-axis like it was
in the previous example, but

01:17:37.000 --> 01:17:39.940
they still all live on the same
line.

01:17:39.940 --> 01:17:40.700
OK?

01:17:40.700 --> 01:17:44.480
What this means is if I give you
a candidate vector like this one

01:17:44.480 --> 01:17:46.240
and said,

01:17:46.980 --> 01:17:50.600
what vector, when I send it
through the machine,

01:17:50.600 --> 01:17:52.780
lives here afterwards,

01:17:52.780 --> 01:17:54.140
the answer is nothing,

01:17:54.140 --> 01:17:56.160
because everything that comes
out of the machine has to live

01:17:56.160 --> 01:17:58.410
on this line.

01:17:58.780 --> 01:18:01.200
What that means is this
transformation is not

01:18:01.200 --> 01:18:01.920
reversible.

01:18:01.920 --> 01:18:04.920
You can't invert it.
Because if it were invertible,

01:18:05.200 --> 01:18:08.420
I should be able to provide any
vector in R2,

01:18:08.420 --> 01:18:11.340
and you should tell me the one
place it had to come from.

01:18:11.340 --> 01:18:12.820
But that's not possible here.

01:18:12.820 --> 01:18:15.500
Because if I pick something not
on the line,

01:18:15.500 --> 01:18:16.640
well, then it didn't come from
anywhere.

01:18:16.640 --> 01:18:18.220
It's not a possible output.

01:18:18.220 --> 01:18:20.300
And if I pick something on the
line, well, there are infinitely

01:18:20.300 --> 01:18:24.740
many ways to have made it.

01:18:24.740 --> 01:18:28.880
Do we see the bigger point I'm
getting at here?

01:18:28.880 --> 01:18:29.860
OK.

01:18:29.860 --> 01:18:35.700
So the big idea is that

01:18:35.700 --> 01:18:37.950
A is invertible

01:18:40.360 --> 01:18:46.260
when the transformation T of x
is equal

01:18:46.260 --> 01:18:49.260
to Ax is invertible.

01:18:53.600 --> 01:18:59.600
So, suppose A is an n by n
matrix,

01:19:00.320 --> 01:19:06.180
A is invertible when,

01:19:08.260 --> 01:19:10.180
given your understanding of what
we've just discussed,

01:19:10.180 --> 01:19:14.100
can you tell me some conditions
that would make A invertible?

01:19:14.100 --> 01:19:19.350
And remember, it's an n by n
matrix.

01:19:20.980 --> 01:19:24.280
Can you tell me one thing that
if I know that that's true,

01:19:24.280 --> 01:19:27.520
then A will be invertible?

01:19:27.520 --> 01:19:28.520
Yeah.

01:19:29.340 --> 01:19:30.340
Good.

01:19:30.440 --> 01:19:35.900
A's columns are linearly
independent.

01:19:40.220 --> 01:19:44.720
Because I have a square matrix
in which I have n columns,

01:19:44.920 --> 01:19:47.700
and each column has n things in
them.

01:19:47.700 --> 01:19:51.080
So I have n column vectors in
Rn.

01:19:51.080 --> 01:19:52.820
If they're linearly independent,

01:19:52.820 --> 01:19:55.820
what is their span?

01:19:57.600 --> 01:20:01.600
If I have seven linearly
independent vectors in R7,

01:20:01.860 --> 01:20:04.820
or two linearly independent
vectors in R2,

01:20:04.820 --> 01:20:07.820
what is their span?

01:20:10.800 --> 01:20:13.800
Or three linearly independent
vectors in R3.

01:20:14.360 --> 01:20:15.500
What is their span?

01:20:15.500 --> 01:20:18.280
All of R3? Yeah, the whole
space.

01:20:18.280 --> 01:20:19.940
Do we agree?

01:20:19.940 --> 01:20:25.220
So if I have A's columns being
linearly independent, that means

01:20:25.220 --> 01:20:28.240
the column space of A is what?

01:20:28.240 --> 01:20:30.490
All of Rn.

01:20:31.100 --> 01:20:34.160
What does that mean? Well, it
means any vector in Rn is a

01:20:34.160 --> 01:20:37.160
possible linear combination of
these columns.

01:20:38.060 --> 01:20:41.440
But also, because A's columns
are linearly independent,

01:20:41.440 --> 01:20:44.120
it means their linear
combinations are unique.

01:20:44.120 --> 01:20:46.580
This is something we focused on
before midterm one.

01:20:46.580 --> 01:20:49.740
If you have building blocks that
are linearly independent,

01:20:49.740 --> 01:20:53.360
then every one of its linear
combinations can only be made in

01:20:53.360 --> 01:20:54.860
one way.

01:20:55.660 --> 01:21:00.100
But if the building blocks are
not linearly independent,

01:21:00.100 --> 01:21:03.920
then any one of its linear
combinations can be made in lots

01:21:03.920 --> 01:21:06.170
of different ways.

01:21:06.960 --> 01:21:11.620
But A's columns being linearly
independent for a square matrix

01:21:11.620 --> 01:21:15.080
is also equivalent to lots of
other things.

01:21:15.080 --> 01:21:15.880
Like,

01:21:15.880 --> 01:21:18.880
A's rows are linearly
independent.

01:21:24.940 --> 01:21:30.190
Or the rank of A is what?

01:21:32.140 --> 01:21:33.520
N, good.

01:21:33.520 --> 01:21:36.400
Or something we talked about
before the break,

01:21:36.400 --> 01:21:41.650
the null space of A is what?

01:21:42.860 --> 01:21:45.500
If the matrix's columns are
linearly dependent, what is the

01:21:45.500 --> 01:21:47.560
matrix's null space?

01:21:47.560 --> 01:21:50.560
Just the 0 vector.

01:21:51.360 --> 01:21:53.200
And there's a few other
equivalent ways of thinking

01:21:53.200 --> 01:21:54.380
about this.

01:21:54.380 --> 01:21:58.130
But these are all equivalent.

01:22:00.080 --> 01:22:02.380
So if any one of these are true,
then they're all true.

01:22:02.380 --> 01:22:04.460
And if any one of them is not
true, then none of them are

01:22:04.460 --> 01:22:05.460
true.

01:22:05.520 --> 01:22:08.860
And we say a matrix is
invertible if any one of these

01:22:08.860 --> 01:22:10.080
things happens.

01:22:10.080 --> 01:22:10.760
Why?

01:22:10.760 --> 01:22:14.510
Well it's because the associated
transformation can be undone.

